WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.816
The next day, the Widow Douglas washed the mud off my clothes.

2
00:00:04.817 --> 00:00:11.279
She didn’t scold me, but she looked so sad that I decided to try to be good for a while.

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.017
I called my father "Pap." No one had seen him for over a year.

4
00:00:16.018 --> 00:00:22.375
That was fine with me. I didn’t want to see Pap again because he beat me.

5
00:00:22.376 --> 00:00:27.767
Then some people in St. Petersburg told me that they’d found his body in the river.

6
00:00:27.768 --> 00:00:34.539
They said the drowned man was just his size, with ragged clothes and long hair like Pap’s.

7
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:41.200
But they really couldn’t recognize the face because the body had been in the water for so long.

8
00:00:41.201 --> 00:00:44.377
They buried the drowned man on the riverbank.

9
00:00:44.378 --> 00:00:47.985
A few days later, though, I thought of something.

10
00:00:47.986 --> 00:00:52.230
The townspeople told me they found the body floating on his back.

11
00:00:52.231 --> 00:00:59.826
But I had once been told that drowned men floated on their faces and only drowned women floated on their backs.

12
00:00:59.827 --> 00:01:05.112
So I decided the body must have been a woman, dressed up in men’s clothes.

13
00:01:05.113 --> 00:01:09.984
After that, I was worried that my father would come back someday.

14
00:01:09.985 --> 00:01:18.719
I still played robbers sometimes with Tom and the other boys, but I was getting bored with it. It was all pretending.

15
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:25.172
On Saturday Tom told us that we were going to ambush some rich merchants who had two hundred elephants,

16
00:01:25.173 --> 00:01:29.952
six hundred camels, and a thousand mules loaded with diamonds.

17
00:01:29.953 --> 00:01:32.648
But that was just a story he made up.

18
00:01:32.649 --> 00:01:36.790
Really we were just ambushing a Sunday school picnic.

19
00:01:36.791 --> 00:01:42.381
We chased the children away, but only got their doughnuts and jam.

20
00:01:42.382 --> 00:01:46.256
"I don’t see any diamonds," I said to Tom.

21
00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:50.481
"Why, they’re all right in front of you," Tom answered.

22
00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:59.000
"And the elephants and camels too. Some magicians must have turned them into a Sunday school picnic before we got here."

23
00:01:59.001 --> 00:02:03.121
Then Tom told me how magicians use genies to help them.

24
00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:07.762
"Let’s get some genies to come and help us ," I

25
00:02:07.763 --> 00:02:12.938
said. Tom explained that you have to rub a magic lamp to make a genie

26
00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:16.609
appear. Then the genie will do whatever you

27
00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.593
want. A few days later I thought I would try

28
00:02:19.594 --> 00:02:26.971
it. I went out into the woods with an old lamp and I rubbed it and rubbed it, but nothing

29
00:02:26.972 --> 00:02:35.412
happened. I think Tom really believed these things, but I

30
00:02:35.413 --> 00:02:39.751
didn’t. Several months passed, and winter came. I went to

31
00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:44.925
school. At first I hated it, but soon I could spell and read and write a

32
00:02:44.926 --> 00:02:52.530
little. I could say the multiplication tables up to six times seven. Then, school became easier for

33
00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:56.062
me. I was also getting used to the Widow Douglas’

34
00:02:56.063 --> 00:03:01.749
house. The widow told me that I was doing well. She said she wasn’t ashamed of

35
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.735
me. One morning at breakfast, I accidentally knocked over the salt

36
00:03:06.736 --> 00:03:10.251
shaker. It is very bad luck to spill

37
00:03:10.252 --> 00:03:17.652
salt. So I reached for some of the salt to throw over my left shoulder because that’s supposed to keep the bad luck

38
00:03:17.653 --> 00:03:27.147
away. But Miss Watson stopped me. "Take your hands away, Huckleberry," she scolded. "What a mess you are always

39
00:03:27.148 --> 00:03:32.395
making!" As I left the house after breakfast, I wondered when my bad luck would

40
00:03:32.396 --> 00:03:35.278
arrive. Then I saw footprints in the new

41
00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:44.525
snow. When I bent down to take a closer look, I noticed a cross made with big nails on the heel of the left boot. I knew that

42
00:03:44.526 --> 00:03:49.182
boot! I ran as fast as I could to Judge Thatcher’s

43
00:03:49.183 --> 00:03:56.240
house. "Why, my boy, you’re all out of breath," he said. "Did you come to get some of your

44
00:03:56.241 --> 00:04:03.264
money?" "No sir," I said. "I don’t want it! I want to give all the money to

45
00:04:03.265 --> 00:04:09.463
you." Judge Thatcher was very surprised. He asked me whether anything was

46
00:04:09.464 --> 00:04:18.281
wrong. "Please take it," I begged him, "and don’t ask me anything—then I won’t have to tell you any lies."

47
00:04:18.282 --> 00:04:23.167
Finally the judge agreed to buy my money from me for one dollar.

48
00:04:23.168 --> 00:04:27.675
He put the dollar in my hand and I left his house.

49
00:04:27.676 --> 00:04:33.010
Miss Watson’s black slave Jim, had a magic hairball as big as your fist.

50
00:04:33.011 --> 00:04:36.279
It had come from the fourth stomach of an ox.

51
00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.643
Jim said there was a spirit inside it that could see the future.

52
00:04:40.644 --> 00:04:48.192
I went to see Jim that evening. I told him that I knew Pap was back and I wanted to know what he would do.

53
00:04:48.193 --> 00:04:54.075
Jim got out his hairball and said some strange words to it that I couldn’t understand.

54
00:04:54.076 --> 00:04:57.521
Then he held it out and dropped it on the floor.

55
00:04:57.522 --> 00:05:04.169
He got down on his knees, put his ear against the hairball, and listened closely. He shook his head.

56
00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:09.072
"The hairball won’t talk without money," Jim said.

57
00:05:09.073 --> 00:05:13.250
I gave Jim an old brass quarter that I had in my pocket.

58
00:05:13.251 --> 00:05:17.601
It wasn’t a real coin, but maybe the hairball would take it.

59
00:05:17.602 --> 00:05:21.424
Jim put the quarter under the hairball and listened again.

60
00:05:21.425 --> 00:05:26.648
This time, Jim said the hairball would tell me my whole fortune.

61
00:05:26.649 --> 00:05:30.843
The hairball talked to Jim, and Jim repeated it to me:

62
00:05:30.844 --> 00:05:37.275
"Your father doesn’t know yet what he’s going to do, but you’re going to be all right.

63
00:05:37.276 --> 00:05:41.751
You’ll have a lot of trouble in your life, but also joy.

64
00:05:41.752 --> 00:05:45.311
And keep away from water as much as you can."

65
00:05:45.312 --> 00:05:51.794
But when I lit my candle and went up to my room that night, there was Pap!

